  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="version-scope" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01 · compatibility and authority</span>
        <h2>Version and execution scope</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Axis</th><th scope="col">Tested version</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></th><td><code>{{ release.version }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Rust provider SDK</th><td><code>dekopon-provider-sdk</code> <code>{{ release.version }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Fake broker for tests</th><td><code>dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit</code> <code>{{ release.version }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Guest bindings</th><td><code>dekopon-provider-http</code>, <code>dekopon-provider-storage</code>, <code>dekopon-provider-clock</code> <code>{{ release.version }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Provider WIT package</th><td><code>dekopon:provider@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Broker HTTP, storage, and clock WIT</th><td><code>dekopon:http@{{ providerSdk.versions.httpWit }}</code> · <code>dekopon:storage@{{ providerSdk.versions.storageWit }}</code> · <code>dekopon:clock@{{ providerSdk.versions.clockWit }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Manifest wire API</th><td><code>{{ providerSdk.versions.manifestApi }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Rust toolchain</th><td><code>{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Composed-world bindings</th><td><code>wit-bindgen</code> <code>{{ providerSdk.versions.witBindgen }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Component tool</th><td><code>wasm-tools</code> <code>{{ providerSdk.versions.wasmTools }}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">WIT package tool</th><td><code>wkg</code> <code>{{ providerSdk.versions.wkg }}</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The host doesn’t negotiate or record the SDK crate version. It strictly decodes manifest and response JSON, requires structurally compatible root exports, and matches each import by its exact WIT identity. Components built against <code>dekopon:provider@0.1.0</code> or <code>0.2.0</code> keep loading. The compatibility proof is loading the finished component: in a test through the fake broker, then in <code>dekopon-brokerd</code>.</p>

      <p>There is one provider host, the broker’s. After Cedar allows an invocation, <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> links exactly the imports the grant names: buffered HTTP, namespace-bound storage, and the grant-free wall clock. An import-free component gets nothing else: no files, environment, network, processes, randomness, or credentials. One v1 capability can’t combine HTTP and storage.</p>

      <p>The build flow is:</p>

      <pre><code>src/lib.rs → wasm32 core module → Component Model component → FakeBroker test → dekopon-brokerd</code></pre>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Wasm isn’t an OS-process boundary.</strong>
        <p>Wasmtime executes components inside the broker process. It doesn’t solve runtime vulnerabilities, side channels, or a hostile process running as the broker’s UID. Production-sandbox claims for Wasmtime are a stated non-goal.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="setup" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02 · pinned prerequisites</span>
        <h2>Install the tested tools</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code>rustup toolchain install {{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} \
  --profile minimal \
  --component rustfmt \
  --component clippy \
  --target wasm32-unknown-unknown

cargo +{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} install wasm-tools --version {{ providerSdk.versions.wasmTools }} --locked</code></pre>

      <p>Nothing else is installed. The fake broker is a dev-dependency, and the real broker is only needed for the load test at the end.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="create-provider" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03 · one import-free capability</span>
        <h2>Create the provider</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code>cargo +{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} new --lib dekopon-text-provider
cd dekopon-text-provider</code></pre>

      <p>Add <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>:</p>

      <pre><code>[toolchain]
channel = "{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }}"
profile = "minimal"
components = ["rustfmt", "clippy"]
targets = ["wasm32-unknown-unknown"]</code></pre>

      <p>Replace <code>Cargo.toml</code>:</p>

      <pre><code>[package]
name = "dekopon-text-provider"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"
rust-version = "{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }}"
publish = false

[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib"]

[dependencies]
dekopon-provider-sdk = "={{ release.version }}"
serde_json = "1"

[dev-dependencies]
dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit = "={{ release.version }}"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread"] }</code></pre>

      <p>Replace <code>src/lib.rs</code>:</p>

      <pre><code>use dekopon_provider_sdk::{
    CapabilityId, EffectKind, Provider, ProviderApiVersion, ProviderCapability, ProviderError,
    ProviderManifest, RiskLevel, export_provider,
};
use serde_json::{Value, json};

struct TextProvider;

impl Provider for TextProvider {
    fn manifest() -&gt; ProviderManifest {
        ProviderManifest {
            api_version: ProviderApiVersion::V1Alpha1,
            id: "text".parse().expect("static provider ID is valid"),
            description: "Transforms text without external I/O".to_owned(),
            command_words: Vec::new(),
            capabilities: vec![ProviderCapability {
                id: "text.uppercase"
                    .parse()
                    .expect("static capability ID is valid"),
                description: "Converts text to Unicode uppercase".to_owned(),
                effect: EffectKind::ReadOnly,
                risk: RiskLevel::Low,
                input_schema: json!({
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "text": {"type": "string"}
                    },
                    "required": ["text"],
                    "additionalProperties": false
                }),
            }],
        }
    }

    fn invoke(capability: &amp;CapabilityId, input: Value) -&gt; Result&lt;Value, ProviderError&gt; {
        if capability.as_str() != "text.uppercase" {
            return Err(ProviderError::new(
                "unsupported-capability",
                format!("text does not implement {capability}"),
            ));
        }

        let fields = input.as_object().ok_or_else(invalid_input)?;
        let text = match (fields.len(), fields.get("text").and_then(Value::as_str)) {
            (1, Some(text)) =&gt; text,
            _ =&gt; return Err(invalid_input()),
        };

        Ok(json!({"text": text.to_uppercase()}))
    }
}

fn invalid_input() -&gt; ProviderError {
    ProviderError::new(
        "invalid-input",
        "input must contain exactly one string field named \"text\"",
    )
}

export_provider!(TextProvider);

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn uppercases_text() {
        let capability = "text.uppercase".parse().expect("valid capability");
        let output = TextProvider::invoke(&amp;capability, json!({"text": "Hello, Straße!"}))
            .expect("valid input succeeds");

        assert_eq!(output, json!({"text": "HELLO, STRASSE!"}));
    }

    #[test]
    fn rejects_malformed_input() {
        let capability = "text.uppercase".parse().expect("valid capability");
        let error = TextProvider::invoke(&amp;capability, json!({"text": 42}))
            .expect_err("invalid input fails");

        assert_eq!(error.code(), "invalid-input");
    }
}</code></pre>

      <p>The JSON Schema is discovery metadata. The host requires an object-shaped schema and an object input; it doesn’t enforce required properties, types, or ranges. Your provider validates those and rejects unknown capability IDs itself. Effect and risk are declarations that Cedar sees and the constraint set must match byte for byte; they don’t prove what the code does.</p>

      <p>Run the native and Wasm checks:</p>

      <pre><code>cargo generate-lockfile
cargo fmt -- --check
cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked

cargo check --locked --target wasm32-unknown-unknown
cargo build --locked --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --release</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section id="build-component" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04 · Component Model boundary</span>
        <h2>Build the component</h2>
      </div>

      <p>The compiler emits a core <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> module with the SDK’s embedded Component Model metadata. <code>wasm-tools component new</code> turns it into the component. The base <code>dekopon:provider@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }}</code> world is:</p>

      <pre><code>world provider {
    export describe: func() -&gt; string;
    export invoke: func(capability: string, input-json: string) -&gt; string;
}</code></pre>

      <p>The same package holds <code>provider-cli</code>, which adds <code>run-command: func(argv: list&lt;string&gt;, stdin: option&lt;string&gt;) -&gt; string</code>, and the older <code>provider-commands</code> with <code>resolve-command</code>. A manifest that declares <code>commandWords</code> must export one of the two; a host that finds both calls <code>run-command</code>. This first build stays on the two-export base world.</p>

      <pre><code>wasm-tools component new \
  target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/dekopon_text_provider.wasm \
  -o text-provider.wasm

wasm-tools validate text-provider.wasm
wasm-tools component wit text-provider.wasm</code></pre>

      <p>The decoded WIT must show <code>describe</code> and <code>invoke</code> and no imports. This <code>wasm-tools</code> output is what proves the final import and export set; nothing in Dekopon inspects a component for you before the host loads it.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Don’t use <code>wasm32-wasip2</code>.</strong>
        <p>It introduces WASI imports the broker host doesn’t link. Any unknown import fails at instantiation, even if the guest never calls it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="run-provider" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05 · the real host in a test</span>
        <h2>Run it against the fake broker</h2>
        <p><code>FakeBroker</code> skips Cedar and the constraint catalog by minting its own authorization. Everything beneath that line is real: the same Wasmtime host, the same limits, the same storage host a deployment runs.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// tests/component.rs</span>
use dekopon_provider_sdk_testkit::FakeBroker;
use serde_json::json;

#[tokio::test(flavor = "multi_thread")]
async fn uppercases_through_the_real_host() {
    let broker = FakeBroker::builder()
        .component("text-provider.wasm")
        .provider("text")
        .build()
        .await
        .expect("component loads");

    let output = broker
        .invoke("text.uppercase", json!({"text": "Hello, Straße!"}))
        .await
        .expect("valid input succeeds");
    assert_eq!(output, json!({"text": "HELLO, STRASSE!"}));

    let error = broker
        .invoke("text.uppercase", json!({"text": 42}))
        .await
        .expect_err("invalid input fails");
    assert_eq!(error.provider_failure().map(|(code, _)| code), Some("invalid-input"));
}</code></pre>

      <p>Build the component before <code>cargo test</code> runs this file, and pass <code>.compile_cache(dir)</code> when a suite loads the same component repeatedly: Cranelift is the whole of a cold start.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Use a multi-thread Tokio test runtime.</strong>
        <p>The storage path dispatches to blocking workers, and a current-thread runtime deadlocks waiting for a namespace lease.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="testkit" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06 · storage in a test</span>
        <h2>Test a storage provider end to end</h2>
        <p>A storage provider needs the host import that exists only when the real component runs. Ask the fake broker for exactly one storage interface and it links the production <code>StorageHost</code> over a temporary root.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code>let broker = FakeBroker::builder()
    .component("my-provider.wasm")
    .provider("my-provider")
    .storage(StorageInterface::DurableFiles, StorageAccess::ReadWrite)
    .build()
    .await?;

broker.invoke("my-provider.write", json!({"value": 1})).await?;
let result = broker.invoke("my-provider.read", json!({})).await?;</code></pre>

      <p>Each call gets a fresh grant with identical scope material, so successive invocations reach one namespace. A quota a test trips here is a quota production would have tripped. The testkit links storage, not HTTP: test an HTTP provider natively with an injected transport, then load-test the component in the broker.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="command-words" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>07 · provider commands inside bash</span>
        <h2>Expose the provider as a shell word</h2>
        <p>The model still receives one tool named <code>bash</code>. A provider can add a word inside that sandbox, so an agent writes <code>uppercase "Hello"</code> instead of assembling a raw capability call, and <code>uppercase --help</code> answers like a real CLI.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Command words use the <code>provider-cli</code> world. Add caller-generated bindings and fetch the exact provider WIT package:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># Cargo.toml</span>
wit-bindgen = { version = "={{ providerSdk.versions.witBindgen }}", default-features = false, features = ["macros", "realloc"] }

<span class="code-comment"># shell</span>
cargo +{{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} install wkg --version {{ providerSdk.versions.wkg }} --locked
mkdir -p wit/deps
wkg get \
  --registry dekopon-agents.github.io \
  --format wit \
  --output wit/deps/provider.wit \
  dekopon:provider@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }}</code></pre>

      <p>Define <code>wit/world.wit</code>:</p>

      <pre><code>package example:text-command@0.1.0;

world provider {
    include dekopon:provider/provider-cli@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }};
}</code></pre>

      <p>Declare the word in the manifest, implement <code>run_command</code>, replace <code>export_provider!</code> with the cli export:</p>

      <pre><code>use dekopon_provider_sdk::CommandRun;

<span class="code-comment">// Inside Provider::manifest()</span>
command_words: vec!["uppercase".to_owned()],

<span class="code-comment">// Inside impl Provider for TextProvider</span>
fn run_command(argv: &amp;[String], stdin: Option&lt;&amp;str&gt;)
    -&gt; Result&lt;CommandRun, ProviderError&gt;
{
    let text = match (argv, stdin) {
        ([flag], _) if flag == "--help" =&gt; {
            return Ok(CommandRun::rendered("usage: uppercase [TEXT]\n", 0));
        }
        ([text], _) =&gt; text.clone(),
        ([], Some(piped)) =&gt; piped.to_owned(),
        _ =&gt; return Ok(CommandRun::rendered_error("usage: uppercase [TEXT]\n", 2)),
    };

    Ok(CommandRun::proposal(
        "text.uppercase".parse().expect("static capability ID is valid"),
        json!({"text": text}),
    ))
}

mod bindings {
    wit_bindgen::generate!({
        path: "wit",
        world: "provider",
        generate_all,
        pub_export_macro: true,
    });
}

dekopon_provider_sdk::export_provider_with_cli!(TextProvider, bindings);</code></pre>

      <p>Regenerate <code>Cargo.lock</code>, then repeat the build, componentization, and WIT inspection from step 04. The component must export <code>describe</code>, <code>invoke</code>, and <code>run-command</code> with zero imports. In a test, <code>broker.run_command("uppercase", &amp;argv, None)</code> returns the <code>CommandRunOutcome</code> the host decoded: a proposal, rendered text with its status, or a decline. A proposal is the rewrite, not its result; pass it to <code>invoke</code> to close the loop.</p>

      <p>The optional <code>clap</code> feature does the argv handling for you: <code>dekopon_provider_sdk::cli::run_command</code> parses against a declared <code>clap::Command</code> tree and renders <code>--help</code>, <code>--version</code>, and usage errors as the upstream tool’s <code>main</code> would, compiled without <code>env</code> or <code>color</code> so no argument reads a process environment and no escape sequence reaches the model.</p>

      <div class="provider-command-journey" role="group" aria-label="Provider command run and authorization">
        <div><small>agent script</small><strong><code>uppercase "Hello"</code></strong><span>familiar command syntax</span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><small>provider run</small><strong><code>text.uppercase</code></strong><span><code>{"text":"Hello"}</code></span></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><small>normal authority path</small><strong>Cedar + constraints</strong><span>allow or deny the proposal</span></div>
      </div>

      <p><code>run-command</code> grants nothing. It runs before authorization, pure and import-free, and a rendered help page charges no capability call. A missing grant still denies the proposal. A declared word that collides with a builtin, control word, or another provider’s word aborts broker startup, every collision listed at once.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="broker-loading" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>08 · startup-installed components</span>
        <h2>How the broker loads providers</h2>
      </div>

      <p><code>dekopon-brokerd</code> loads components before it listens: either the local files <code>providers:</code> names, or the exact-reference OCI set that <code>dekopon-brokerd provider sync</code> materialized into a lock and a store beforehand. The daemon itself performs no registry request, and there is no request-time loading or hot reload; restart the broker to load replaced bytes.</p>

      <p>Every load, manifest, or command-word conflict failure aborts startup. The broker compiles providers in parallel with an optional persistent Wasmtime compilation cache, retains linked instance templates, and still gives each description, command run, and invocation a fresh store and instance. A locked component’s length and SHA-256 are compared against the same read buffer Wasmtime consumes. A digest proves byte identity, not publisher identity: the manager verifies no attestation.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="broker-http" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>09 · one broker-owned host import</span>
        <h2>Add broker-mediated HTTP</h2>
        <p>The build delta for an HTTP provider.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code>dekopon-provider-sdk = "={{ release.version }}"
dekopon-provider-http = "={{ release.version }}"
wit-bindgen = { version = "={{ providerSdk.versions.witBindgen }}", default-features = false, features = ["macros", "realloc"] }</code></pre>

      <pre><code>wkg get \
  --registry dekopon-agents.github.io \
  --format wit \
  --output wit/deps/http.wit \
  dekopon:http@{{ providerSdk.versions.httpWit }}</code></pre>

      <pre><code>package example:text-http@0.1.0;

world provider {
    include dekopon:provider/provider@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }};
    import dekopon:http/client@{{ providerSdk.versions.httpWit }};
}</code></pre>

      <pre><code>mod bindings {
    wit_bindgen::generate!({
        path: "wit",
        world: "provider",
        generate_all,
        pub_export_macro: true,
    });
}

dekopon_provider_sdk::export_provider_with_bindings!(YourProvider, bindings);</code></pre>

      <p><code>dekopon-provider-http</code> supplies request and response types plus the imported <code>send</code> call. It contains no transport, credential, destination policy, or ambient I/O. Generated bindings contain generated <code>unsafe</code>, so a composed provider can’t apply a crate-wide <code>#![forbid(unsafe_code)]</code>.</p>

      <h3>Who controls an HTTP request?</h3>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Broker and native host</th><th scope="col">Provider code</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Enforce the exact authority and case-sensitive method grants, and <code>maxRequests</code> per invocation.</td><td>Chooses paths, queries, bodies, and permitted ordinary headers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Enforce request and response byte limits per call, header syntax and count, and the fixed forbidden-header list.</td><td>Validates every input and interpolated path segment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Require public-address HTTPS or explicitly allowed loopback plaintext; disable redirects, proxies, decompression, and cookies.</td><td>Uses fixed request shapes instead of URL, method, or body passthrough.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Inject one destination-bound <code>Authorization</code> value after validating guest headers, and refuse a response that echoes it.</td><td>Can’t select, import, or read the credential value.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Cedar sees the trusted principal and routing context, the provider and capability IDs, and the effect and risk classifications. It never sees invocation input, paths, queries, bodies, credentials, or limits, so it can’t say “only repositories under this organization”; scope the token instead. The broker can’t infer whether a path or body performs a mutation.</p>

      <p>The audit record carries the sanitized HTTP evidence: method, authority, status, accounted bytes, and whether a credential was injected. It omits paths, queries, headers, and bodies, and it isn’t proof of an exact remote mutation; a remote effect isn’t rolled back after a later trap or timeout.</p>

      <p>Default broker-host ceilings: a 10-million-unit fuel budget, 30 seconds of wall time, 1 MiB of invocation input and provider output, 64 MiB per memory, and 256 MiB across every concurrent store. HTTP ceilings: 32 calls, 1 MiB per request, 4 MiB per response. The independently published <a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon-provider-gh"><code>gh</code> provider</a> is the fixed-request-shape example to copy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="capability-matrix" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>10 · provider work versus platform work</span>
        <h2>Capability matrix</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Current classification</th><th scope="col">Required work</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Import-free computation, in a test or through the broker</th><td><strong>Supported</strong></td><td>The artifact, plus for the broker an operator’s constraint set, Cedar policy, and routing.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Narrow public-HTTPS reads or writes with a destination-bound <code>Authorization</code></th><td><strong>Supported; provider plus operator work</strong></td><td>Compose the HTTP world; configure the exact authority, methods, limits, policy, and credential. No broker source change.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Namespace-bound JSONL or durable-file storage</th><td><strong>Supported; advanced</strong></td><td>Use <code>dekopon-provider-storage</code> with exactly one interface; configure storage limits, constraints, policy, and a retained volume. Not combinable with HTTP in one capability.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Command words with real CLI behavior</th><td><strong>Supported</strong></td><td>Include <code>provider-cli</code>, implement <code>run_command</code>, use <code>export_provider_with_cli!</code>, and choose a word that collides with nothing.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Wall-clock time</th><td><strong>Supported</strong></td><td><code>dekopon-provider-clock::now_unix_millis</code> during <code>invoke</code> only; a read during <code>describe</code> or a command run traps.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Host paths, processes, sockets, SSH, database wire protocols, randomness, private-network or proxy-only APIs, streaming, path-level Cedar policy, mTLS, request signing</th><td><strong>Unsupported</strong></td><td>New host interfaces plus policy, constraints, and audit changes. Adding an import to your world can’t extend the shipped linker.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="distribution" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>11 · operator-owned supply chain</span>
        <h2>Package and install it yourself</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Dekopon provides no publication service for out-of-tree providers; the <code>wkg</code> registry distributes WIT packages, not implementations. Two install paths exist, both operator-owned. An exact-reference OCI set, pinned by tag or manifest digest, that <code>dekopon-brokerd provider sync</code> resolves into a lock and a store:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="code-comment"># providers.yaml</span>
apiVersion: dekopon.dev/provider-set/v1alpha1
providers:
  - source: ghcr.io/dekopon-agents/provider-gh:0.1.0
  - source: ghcr.io/example/text-provider@sha256:0123…cdef</code></pre>

      <p>Or a derived image that places the file where <code>broker.yaml</code> names it:</p>

      <pre><code>FROM {{ release.imageReference }}@sha256:&lt;verified-index-digest&gt;

COPY --chown=65532:65532 --chmod=0644 \
  text-provider.wasm \
  /opt/dekopon/providers/text-provider.wasm

USER 65532:65532</code></pre>

      <p>For provider CI: pin the compiler, dependencies, WIT files, and <code>wasm-tools</code>; commit <code>Cargo.lock</code>; run the native tests and the fake-broker test; componentize the exact release artifact and inspect its final imports and exports; publish the SHA-256 and a provenance attestation. The independently released providers do exactly this from their own repositories.</p>

      <p>Published chart <code>{{ release.chart.publishedVersion }}</code> declares <code>appVersion: {{ release.chart.publishedAppVersion }}</code>; chart <code>{{ release.chart.sourceVersion }}</code>, naming app <code>{{ release.chart.sourceAppVersion }}</code>, exists in source and isn’t published. The chart places files and processes; it doesn’t infer provider authority. Record the image digest and the provider artifact SHA outside the audit record, which names the policy that authorized a call and not the build that executed it.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="coding-agent-prompt" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>12 · copy, adapt, verify</span>
        <h2>Prompt a coding agent</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Replace the bracketed fields, paste it into your coding agent, and review its code and command output before you trust the artifact.</p>

      <pre><code>Build a Rust Dekopon provider for [behavior]. Use provider ID [provider-id]
and capability ID [provider-id.capability].

Target the {{ release.tag }} pre-production contract as one pinned set:
- Rust {{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} in rust-toolchain.toml, targeting wasm32-unknown-unknown
- dekopon-provider-sdk = "={{ release.version }}"
- dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit = "={{ release.version }}" as a dev-dependency
- dekopon:provider@{{ providerSdk.versions.providerWit }} and dekopon.dev/provider/v1alpha1
- wasm-tools {{ providerSdk.versions.wasmTools }}
- dekopon-brokerd {{ release.version }} for the final load test

Keep the component import-free. Leave command_words empty and export with
export_provider!. Do not use WASI. Validate the capability ID and every input
field in provider code; the manifest JSON Schema is discovery metadata, not
host validation. Add native tests for accepted and malformed input, and one
#[tokio::test(flavor = "multi_thread")] that loads the component through
FakeBroker and asserts the accepted output and the invalid-input failure.

Keep Cargo.lock under version control. Run and report these gates:
- cargo generate-lockfile
- cargo fmt -- --check
- cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings
- cargo check --locked --target wasm32-unknown-unknown
- cargo build --locked --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --release
- wasm-tools component new on the release core module
- wasm-tools validate and wasm-tools component wit on the final component
- cargo test --locked, including the FakeBroker test

Require exactly the describe and invoke exports and zero imports.

Do not add a host import, networking, credentials, files, processes, or broker
changes to satisfy [behavior]. If [behavior] needs I/O, stop and describe that
as separate work. Broker-mediated HTTP requires
  dekopon-provider-http = "={{ release.version }}",
  wit-bindgen = "={{ providerSdk.versions.witBindgen }}",
  dekopon:http@{{ providerSdk.versions.httpWit }}, and wkg {{ providerSdk.versions.wkg }},
plus a provider-owned composed world and operator-owned constraints, Cedar
policy, credentials, installation, and routing. Broker-owned storage requires
  dekopon-provider-storage = "={{ release.version }}" and dekopon:storage@{{ providerSdk.versions.storageWit }},
exactly one JSONL or durable-files import, and operator-owned storage
constraints, retained volume, policy, and routing. Any other import or
enforcement need is a host/platform change.</code></pre>
